Green-composed "Albatross" song "Albatross" brought the group's first number one hit and catapulted it to the number of the British music scene.
Yet the swift climb soon revealed its dark pages: Green didn't cope the media hype, his experiments with the drug LSD became increasingly extreme.
He later commented on life between music and addiction in an interview: "The worst thing happened to me in life are drugs - the best thing happening was to land a number 1 hit."
At the end of the decade in 1979 Green surprisingly succeeded in achieving respect with an album inspired by religious themes.
Meanwhile Fleetwood swam a wave of success without him in a new formation.
Her 1977 album "Rumours" sold more less than 40 million copies worldwide.
Despite several small solo success, Green didn't achieve a bigger comeback until he restarted in 1996 with a fellow musician.
At the very beginning, the British "Daily Telegraph" spoke about a rock star returning to earth.
Out of drugs Green released a number of albums with Peter Green Splinter Group until the band dissipated in 2004.
For music experts already during his years, he was a blues-lishing legend - and with his gentle appendacle one of the most influential guitarists of recent decades.
